{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775330908-kNfUdCLjl0w8EIjrV6Ym","role":"assistant","created":1775330915,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The historical exchange rate data from 1949-1952 is not easily accessible online through these APIs. Given that these were historical rates during the Bretton Woods era, I know the fixed exchange rate was $2.80 = £1 (or equivalently, £1 = $2.80, which means the GBP per USD rate would be approximately 0.3571).\n\nBut I need to verify this or find a source. Let me try one more search approach.\n\nActually, wait - let me re-read the problem. The question asks for:\n- GBP per USD (pounds per $1) exchange rate\n- Arithmetic mean of monthly rates for that calendar year\n\nThe Bretton Woods system established after WWII set the pound sterling at $2.80 per £1, meaning £1 = $2.80, so GBP per USD = 1/2.80 = 0.3571.\n\nBut this was a fixed rate. During 1949-1951 there might have been some fluctuation. Let me try to search for more specific data.\n\nActually, I recall that:\n- In 1949, the UK devalued the pound from $4.03 to $2.80\n- This rate remained fixed until 1971\n\nSo for the years 1949, 1950, 1951, the official exchange rate would be £0.3571 per $1 (or 2.80 USD per GBP).\n\nLet me verify this by checking if I can find any source mentioning these rates.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_def39e1c7ad44229bf572a94","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://www.britannica.com/money/United-Kingdom-currency\" 2>/dev/null | grep -i \"2.80\\|devaluation\\|pound\" | head -10"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
