{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775330957-mkCG1An7cQ4rhAFXpack","role":"assistant","created":1775330963,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I found it! The pound was devalued by 30% on September 19, 1949 against the US dollar. \n\nSo the exchange rate before devaluation was around $4.03 per £1 (the pre-war rate), and after devaluation it became around $2.80 per £1.\n\nFor the three periods:\n- 12-month period ending Feb 28, 1950 → calendar year 1949 → the devaluation happened in Sept 1949\n- 12-month period ending Feb 28, 1951 → calendar year 1950 → use 1950 rate\n- 12-month period ending Feb 29, 1952 → calendar year 1951 → use 1951 rate\n\nNow I need to find the exact annual average exchange rates for those years. Let me try to find historical data.\n\nSince I can't access FRED or the Bank of England directly, I need to find another way. Let me search for specific historical data.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_ecdde75d8e02407ebf699f1f","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exchange_rate\" 2>/dev/null | grep -A20 \"1949\\|1950\\|1951\" | head -40"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
